    slug: 'new-york-attyx-rooftop-solar-predatory-lending-scheme',
    description: `Attyx, LLC — formerly operating as SUNco Capital and later rebranding under aliases including "LGCY Power" — promised low-income and elderly New York homeowners free or reduced-cost roof repairs, HVAC work, and other home improvements in exchange for installing rooftop solar systems, often citing supposed government "incentive" programs. Sales representatives used electronic tablets to rush customers through what was described as an application form but was actually a binding loan agreement, locking homeowners into financing — arranged through lenders including Solar Mosaic and WebBank — for solar systems priced far above their real cost, with lender fees buried inside the inflated price. New York Attorney General Letitia James sued Attyx, its lenders, and related entities in New York State Supreme Court on March 17, 2026, alleging the company generated approximately $275 million from more than 4,500 New York City-area solar installations through this pattern and kept soliciting and installing systems even after the New York Public Service Commission revoked its operating license in late 2025, prompting the rebrand. The Attorney General's office said it had received more than 200 consumer complaints. "When New Yorkers need home repairs, they should be able to get quality service without worrying about scammers," James said, and her office is seeking restitution and damages for affected consumers, voiding of the Attyx and lender agreements, an injunction against further violations, and civil penalties. New York homeowners approached about a free or discounted solar or home-improvement offer are advised to read every document before signing regardless of what a salesperson calls it, to independently verify any claimed government incentive program with the actual agency before relying on it, and to check a solar installer's license status with the Public Service Commission before agreeing to financing.`,
