    slug: 'california-fake-court-notice-text-email-scam',
    description: `California Attorney General Rob Bonta issued a consumer alert on March 30, 2026, warning residents about a wave of fraudulent text messages and emails impersonating California courts. The messages falsely claim the recipient has an outstanding traffic or toll violation and must pay a fine or make a court appearance immediately to avoid escalating penalties, often formatted with a QR code or hyperlink designed to look like it leads to an official court payment portal. "Californians, beware of scam notices claiming to come from California courts and asking you to make a traffic or toll ticket payment or face the consequences. Courts will never text, call, or email to ask for money," Bonta said. The Attorney General's office says real California courts do not initiate contact by text or email demanding immediate payment, and residents who receive one of these notices should not scan the QR code, click any link, or pay anything, and should instead verify any claimed case or ticket directly through the court's official website at courts.ca.gov or by calling the court using a phone number looked up independently rather than one supplied in the message. Anyone who already clicked a malicious link or paid a fraudulent notice is urged to dispute any resulting charges with their bank or card issuer and report the scam to the California Attorney General's office at oag.ca.gov/report.`,
