    slug: 'florida-cyber-fraud-enforcement-unit-crypto-recovery',
    description: `Florida Attorney General James Uthmeier's Office of Statewide Prosecution announced on May 31, 2026 that its Cyber Fraud Enforcement Unit (CFEU) had recovered and returned $229,096 in stolen cryptocurrency to victims of four separate scams across the state, illustrating both the scale and the variety of crypto fraud investigators are now confronting. In one Sarasota County case, a 69-year-old man reported in October 2025 that a caller posing as a crypto investment advisor had directed him into a fake trading app showing a fabricated $3.2 million balance, then demanded an additional $325,000 in "release fees" before he could withdraw anything, in the course of losing $970,000; investigators recovered $139,400 of it through blockchain tracing. In St. Johns County, a 77-year-old woman was told over four months beginning in July 2025 that her bank account had been compromised by callers posing as PayPal employees, who walked her through converting her savings into cryptocurrency across 17 separate transactions at Bitcoin ATMs; in a separate St. Johns County case, a woman met a man claiming to work at Goldman Sachs on the dating app Coffee Meets Bagel, was moved to WhatsApp, and was walked into a rigged crypto trading platform showing fabricated profits before being told she owed a $59,000 "tax" to withdraw — a classic "pig butchering" pattern. "Cyber criminals are often targeting our most vulnerable populations, including seniors," Uthmeier said, adding that the unit is "setting the standard for cryptocurrency fraud recovery nationwide." Floridians contacted by a stranger — whether through a dating app, a cold call, or a message claiming their bank account is at risk — who is steered toward moving money into cryptocurrency should treat that instruction alone as a red flag, and can report a suspected crypto scam to Florida's Cyber Fraud Enforcement Unit through the Attorney General's Office at MyFloridaLegal.com.`,
