    slug: 'dc-fake-toll-collection-text-scam',
    description: `District of Columbia Attorney General Brian Schwalb issued a consumer alert on May 13, 2025 warning residents about a wave of fraudulent text messages impersonating government toll collection agencies. The messages falsely claim the recipient has an unpaid toll balance and must "settle the balance within the next 12 hours to avoid severe penalties," threatening vehicle registration suspension, wage garnishment, or a lowered credit score, and include a link to a fake tolling website built to harvest personal information and credit card numbers. Schwalb's office noted the scam traces to a broader pattern the FBI has linked to Chinese cybercrime syndicates, which generated more than 59,000 complaints nationwide in 2024 alone. "Toll enforcement agencies will never contact you via text to demand payment," Schwalb said, warning the messages are designed to "steal District residents' personal information and hard-earned money." Residents who receive one of these texts are advised never to click the link, to contact their bank immediately if they already entered payment information, to consider a temporary credit freeze with the major credit bureaus, and to verify any real toll balance directly through the relevant agency — such as Virginia's E-ZPass program at (877) 762-7824 or Maryland's E-ZPass program at (888) 321-6824 — using a number looked up independently rather than one in the text. Suspected scams can be reported to the FTC at ReportFraud.ftc.gov.`,
