    slug: 'montana-missoula-fake-amazon-federal-agent-gold-pickup',
    description: `In April 2025, callers posing as Amazon customer service representatives contacted an elderly victim in Missoula, Montana and told her that her identity had been stolen and her accounts compromised, then transferred her to someone claiming to be a federal agent who said the only way to protect her money and gold was to hand it over in person for "safekeeping." Believing the ruse, the victim allowed a courier to come to her home on multiple occasions to collect cash and gold bullion, ultimately losing more than $900,000 before law enforcement intervened and arrested the courier, Zabi Ullah Mohammed, when he returned to her house a final time. The Missoula case led investigators to a much larger network: prosecutors say Mohammed and his co-conspirators used the same "Amazon account compromised, hand your assets to a federal agent" script against elderly victims around the country, causing total losses of more than $3.9 million, with Mohammed personally retrieving approximately $1,700,933 in gold and U.S. currency as a courier. Following a trial, a federal jury found Mohammed guilty in October 2025 of conspiracy to commit wire fraud and wire fraud, and the U.S. Attorney's Office for the District of Montana announced he was sentenced to six years (72 months) in federal prison followed by three years of supervised release. Prosecutors and Montana law enforcement stress that no legitimate company like Amazon and no real federal law enforcement agency will ever call to say your identity was stolen and then send a courier to your home to physically pick up cash or gold for "protection" — that pickup is the theft, and anyone who receives such a call should hang up, contact Amazon and local police directly using numbers they look up themselves, and never hand cash, gold, or other valuables to a stranger who shows up at the door.`,
